"ensayos","from":{"books":[],"podcasts":[{"name":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","n":2}],"lists":[],"n_lists":0}}],"members":[{"id":96164110355701,"type":"artist","name":"Mel Taylor (drums)","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":22431574689821,"type":"artist","name":"Mel Taylor","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"}],"member_of":[{"id":127845433913067,"type":"artist","name":"Mel Taylor - The Ventures","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"}],"genres":[{"id":226765575714711,"type":"genre","name":"Surf Music","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":261,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":159303501244351,"type":"genre","name":"instrumental","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":48,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"}],"album_genres":[{"id":228838194452277,"type":"genre","name":"Surf Rock","n":24},{"id":226765575714711,"type":"genre","name":"Surf Music","n":11},{"id":53681850040905,"type":"genre","name":"Rock & Roll","n":4},{"id":246667248684223,"type":"genre","name":"Psychsploitation","n":1},{"id":187212753338240,"type":"genre","name":"Twist","n":1},{"id":157953610416146,"type":"genre","name":"Swamp Rock","n":1},{"id":56256825844371,"type":"genre","name":"Latin Rock","n":1}],"labels":[],"album_labels":[],"own_scenes":[],"scenes":[{"id":270959780819050,"name":"Los rockers de garaje","book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","start":1963,"end":1967,"desc":"En 1966 no tenían nombre (Billboard los llamaba «rockers»); hoy se les llama grupos de garaje. La respuesta creativa americana a la invasión británica: chicos blancos de suburbio que ensayaban en el garaje familiar y tocaban en gimnasios, iglesias y fiestas, con cortes de pelo de Brian Jones, actitud de Jagger, fuzz, rave-ups a lo Yardbirds, Them y Stones y, casi siempre, un órgano Farfisa o Vox con un regusto siniestro de película de terror de autocine. Recuperaban la línea del rock americano de 1963-1964 (Kingsmen, Trashmen) que los Beatles cortaron en seco: «Hanky Panky» de Tommy James, grabado en 1963, fue número 1 en julio de 1966. Ese septiembre las listas se llenaron de discos duros: «96 Tears» de ? and the Mysterians (mexicanoamericanos de Flint, con un órgano hipnótico de otro disco), «Dirty Water» de los Standells, «You’re Gonna Miss Me» de los 13th Floor Elevators y «Psychotic Reaction» de Count Five, calco del «I’m a Man» de los Yardbirds. Quejas dirigidas a las mujeres, pero también una nueva confianza; quejas pequeñas que empezaban a encontrar un objetivo, como en «One of These Days» de los Roosters.","color":"#f97316","family":"Garage y punk americano","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/#garage-rockers"},{"id":169050477627223,"name":"Antes del diluvio","book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","start":1957,"end":1962,"desc":"La «edad oscura» del rock'n'roll: los rebeldes son sustituidos por ídolos adolescentes educados y el Brill Building devuelve el poder a los compositores profesionales. Pero en esos años se incuban la revolución futura: la guitarra fuzz de Link Wray, los bailes de moda (el twist), el rock instrumental de guitarras (Duane Eddy, Ventures, Shadows), el calipso y el primer ska, el doo-wop más inventivo (Coasters, Drifters, Five Royales), el nacimiento del soul (Sam Cooke, Ray Charles, Motown, Stax), el estudio como instrumento de Joe Meek y el surf de los Beach Boys y del «muro de sonido» de Phil Spector.","color":"#f59e0b","family":"Raíces: rock'n'roll, country y roots","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#before-flood"}],"book_tracks":[{"id":27893299383198,"artist":"Ventures","artist_id":252507541103972,"title":"Walk Don't Run","year":1960,"ess":0,"yt":"hIuIIqbyEIU","kind":"s","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Antes del diluvio","scene_id":169050477627223,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#before-flood","chapters":["Sección 2.3"],"pages":[[1,18,null]]},{"id":49010788854027,"artist":"The Ventures","artist_id":252507541103972,"title":"Secret Agent Man","year":1966,"ess":1,"yt":"oibluitvYkc","kind":null,"book":{"id":1,"title":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/"},"scene":"Los rockers de garaje","scene_id":270959780819050,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/1966/#garage-rockers","chapters":["Discografía: Los singles de cada mes de 1966"],"pages":[[1,392]]}],"mentions":[{"book":"1966: The Year the Decade Exploded","author":"Jon Savage","page":392,"text":"…Chris Farlowe, ‘Think’, Immediate David Bowie with the Lower Third, ‘Can’t Help Thinking About Me’, Pye The Olympics, ‘Secret Agents’, Mirwood The Ventures, ‘Secret Agent Man’, Dolton Tom Jones, ‘Thunderball’, Decca Cilla Black, ‘Love’s Just a Broken Heart’, Parlophone Bob Lind, ‘Elusive Butterfly’,…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":18,"text":"…and Forty Miles Of Bad Road (1959), and began a trend towards more and more atmospheric music, such as Santo & Johnny's Sleep Walk (1959), the Ventures' Walk Don't Run (1960) and (in Britain) the Shadows' Apache (1960). Even the drums were employed as lead instrument in instrumental rock, as was the…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":186,"text":"…who internalized Roy Orbison's romantic crooning, Elvis Presley's sobbing tenor, languid lounge music, Ennio Morricone's epic soundtracks, and the Ventures' atmospheric instrumentals. The melancholy and stoic mood of Silvertone (1985), well rendered by subterranean guitar strumming, revealed an…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":243,"text":"…Road Racing (1994) by the Phantom Surfers in San Francisco. In Canada, Mark Brodie And The Beaver Patrol resurrected the vibrato melodies of the Ventures and Dick Dale on The Shores Of Hell (1996), thus following in the footsteps of Shadowy Men On A Shadowy Planet. Shark Quest (1), in North Carolina,…"}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"Early Thematic Instrumental Albums (1961)","body":"In 1961, this instrumental band from California began releasing records built around specific themes, such as space, surfing, country music, and even TV themes. \"In 1961, an instrumental band from California called The Venture started releasing records built around themes like space and surfing and country music and even TV themes.\"","year":1961,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Alt Rock Concept Albums: Part 1","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Dm5N6yDRxXQ"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Cover of \"Honky Tonk\"","body":"The Ventures, as an established instrumental act, covered Bill Doggett's \"Honky Tonk,\" further solidifying its status as a staple for instrumental bands. \"It became a staple for cover bands, and it was recorded by the obvious instrumental acts like the ventures.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 50: \"Honky Tonk\", by Bill Doggett","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=H2rYnKmeZF0"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Influential Instrumental Group","body":"The Ventures were an instrumental group in the US whose hits with pure instrumental records were crucial in the development of rock and roll guitar bands. \"Indeed we probably wouldn't have rock and roll guitar bands at all, without instrumental groups like the Ventures in the US.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"A History of Rock Music in 500 Songs","episode":"Episode 50: \"Honky Tonk\", by Bill Doggett","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=H2rYnKmeZF0"}]}],"awards":[{"kind":"award","title":"Rock and Roll Hall of Fame (2008)","body":"Won.","year":2008,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q179191"}]}],"conc